### Link to repro

https://playground.react.dev/#

### Repro steps

When opening the playground, you should see this error:

```
Found 1 error:

Error: Cannot access refs during render

React refs are values that are not needed for rendering. Refs should only be accessed outside of render, such as in event handlers or effects. Accessing a ref value (the `current` property) during render can cause your component not to update as expected (https://react.dev/reference/react/useRef).

  4 |         ref.current = init();
  5 |     }
> 6 |     return ref.current;
    |            ^^^^^^^^^^^ Cannot access ref value during render
  7 | }
  8 |
  9 | export default function MyApp() {
```

To explain a bit this code:

```js
function useConstant(init) {
    const ref = React.useRef(undefined);
    if (ref.current === undefined) {
        ref.current = init();
    }
    return ref.current;
}
```

I know that it breaks the rule of hooks in a way. But when reading [`useRef()`](https://react.dev/reference/react/useRef#avoiding-recreating-the-ref-contents) documentation, we can see this:

> Normally, writing or reading `ref.current` during render is not allowed. However, it’s fine in this case because the result is always the same, and the condition only executes during initialization so it’s fully predictable.

Checking for `ref.current === undefined` & `ref.current = init()` in this code snippet is considered as ok by the compiler, but not when reading just after `ref.current`. Shouldn't it be considered as okay too?

